            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Handel in England</strong><br>\nHandel settled in England in 1712 and became a naturalized British subject in 1727. He initially wrote for the royalty, composing \"Water Music\" for King George I. His move to England marked the beginning of a prosperous career composing operas and other works that were influenced by both German and Italian styles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Royal Commissions and the 'Water Music'</strong><br>\nThe \"Water Music\" suites were composed by Handel for a royal boat party down the Thames River in 1717. These pieces showcased Handel's mastery of orchestration and were written to please King George I. The king was so pleased that he ordered the suites to be played three times during the trip.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Opera House Manager</strong><br>\nHandel was also an opera manager, bringing Italian opera to the British audience. He managed the Royal Academy of Music and later the Covent Garden Theatre. However, managing operas was financially risky, and Handel faced bankruptcy several times due to this venture.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
