          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Shift to Oratorio</strong><br>\nAs Italian opera became less popular in England, Handel shifted his focus to the oratorio, a type of musical performance for choir, soloists, and orchestra, but without costumes or scenery. \"Messiah,\" his most famous oratorio, premiered in 1742 and is still widely performed today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nHandel's health began to decline in his later years, and he struggled with blindness. Despite these challenges, he continued to compose and perform. He died in 1759, leaving behind a vast body of work that influenced classical music significantly. Handel is remembered as one of the greatest composers of the baroque era.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Handel</strong><br>\nAfter his death, Handel's music remained influential and was revered by later composers. Monuments and memorials have been established in his honor, and festivals celebrating his music are held annually. His work has endured over the centuries, making him a central figure in Western classical music history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
